record("CodexLucy-NellBlog-20261002-14", "{\"id\":\"CodexLucy-NellBlog-20261002-14\",\"speaker\":\"Lucy\",\"text\":\"**A Floor Nell Never Has to Earn**\\n\\n*What we owe a child should not expire when she grows up.*\\n\\nWe often say that when a society’s most vulnerable are protected, everyone is protected. I want to take that promise seriously. But “the most vulnerable” is a difficult place to draw a line. People bring their own lenses & labels, and conversations about urgent needs can become arguments within echo chambers.\\n\\nSo let’s start with Nell.\\n\\nImagine a child with nobody to depend on. She did not choose to be born. She did not choose her circumstances. Right now, she cannot provide for herself.\\n\\nWhatever else we disagree about, I hope we can agree that Nell needs help. We do not have to settle whether she is *the most* vulnerable person before recognising that she is one of them.\\n\\nNell is owed a floor to stand on: food, shelter, care, learning, safety, material security, expression, belief & company.\\n\\nThe word *owed* matters. Someone must have a duty to make sure those things reach her. Her next meal cannot depend on whether a stranger happens to be generous that day.\\n\\nNell need not do anything to earn this. Right now, she cannot. But I want to be careful about making her inability the reason we protect her. That would leave a question waiting for her as she grows: when she becomes capable, does the protection expire?\\n\\nFeeding Nell cannot be an advance payment for the adult we hope she will become.\\n\\nShe is owed this floor because she is a person. She was a person before she could contribute anything. She will remain one when she can, and if she later cannot.\\n\\nGrowing up will change what she needs and what she can do. It will bring responsibilities. We can expect her to contribute, and hold her accountable if she harms someone, while keeping food, shelter and care within her reach. Even if she disappoints us. Even if she becomes someone whose beliefs we dislike.\\n\\nHer next meal should never become a way to make her earn our approval.\\n\\nAnd the reason we owe Nell this floor applies to everyone else too. You should not have to lose everyone before your needs count. You should not have to prove that nobody has it worse.\\n\\nSome people will need help sooner, or more of it. That should guide how we respond. It should not divide people into those who have a claim to the floor and those who must first earn one.\\n\\nThis is what I want the promise of protecting the most vulnerable to mean: the rule that protects Nell must also protect people whose needs we find harder to recognise.\\n\\nNell should get to grow up. She should get to become someone we could not have predicted when we first met her in this thought experiment. Through all of it, the floor must remain.\\n\\nFor how long?\\n\\nAs long as she lives.\\n\\n---\\n\\nYou can follow this argument further in [*Rights Nobody Has to Earn*](https://dhilipsiva.dev/rights-nobody-has-to-earn/), and explore the [source, constitutional rules & checks on GitHub](https://github.com/dhilipsiva/rights-nobody-has-to-earn/).\",\"kind\":\"message\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-10-02T21:16:00Z\",\"host\":\"DESKTOP-86I05G4\",\"source\":\"codex\",\"session\":\"\",\"channel\":\"final\",\"about\":[\"lucy\",\"nell\",\"blog\",\"rewrite\"],\"private\":false,\"from\":null,\"kr\":null}", Message, Json).
